Abstract
We present a full theoretical design and mathematical analysis of the Nested Conical Bowl (NCB), a surface‑integrated centrifuge that superimposes Earth‑normal gravity onto the Martian environment. The rotating floor is a paraboloid of revolution derived by solving the Diagonal Gravity Problem; all structural, dynamic, and life‑support subsystems are developed from first principles and conservative engineering allowances. This version excludes any dependence on machine‑learning training or physical testing. Every component is derived analytically, and stability proofs are given via Lyapunov methods for a parameter‑scheduled robust controller. All mathematics is presented at the level of formal proof, and a minimum safety factor of 4.0 is maintained throughout.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 The Diagonal Gravity Problem
On a planetary surface, artificial gravity generated by rotation adds a horizontal centripetal component to the local vertical gravitational acceleration. The net acceleration vector at a radial coordinate r in the rotating frame is

\mathbf{g}_{\text{net}}(r) \;=\; \begin{pmatrix} \omega^2 r \\[2pt] -g_M \end{pmatrix},

where \omega is the angular velocity of the habitat and g_M = 3.72076\,\text{m/s}^2 is the Martian surface gravity. The direction of \mathbf{g}_{\text{net}} depends on r:

\tan\theta(r) = \frac{\omega^2 r}{g_M}.

For a floor to be everywhere perpendicular to the net gravity, its slope must equal \tan\theta(r) at every point. No surface of revolution with constant cross‑sectional slope (cone, cylinder) can satisfy this condition; this is the Diagonal Gravity Problem.

1.2 Paraboloid Solution
Integrating \frac{dy}{dr} = \frac{\omega^2 r}{g_M} gives the unique profile

\boxed{y(r) = \frac{\omega^2}{2g_M}\,r^2 + y_0}.

Setting the vertex at y(0)=0 yields y_0=0. This is a paraboloid of revolution. The centripetal acceleration required at the rim r_{\max}=50\,\text{m} to produce a resultant of g_E = 9.81\,\text{m/s}^2 (Earth equivalent) is

a_c = \sqrt{g_E^2 - g_M^2} \approx 9.08\,\text{m/s}^2.

Hence the design angular velocity is

\omega = \sqrt{\frac{a_c}{r_{\max}}} \approx 0.4260\,\text{rad/s},
\qquad
T = \frac{2\pi}{\omega} \approx 14.76\,\text{s},
\qquad
\text{RPM} \approx 4.07.

The rim rises to y(50) \approx 61.0\,\text{m}. The complete bowl surface is described by y = k r^2 with

k = \frac{\omega^2}{2g_M} \approx 0.02439\,\text{m}^{-1}.

1.3 Scope
All subsequent sections provide fully analytical derivations and conservative design choices. The only numerical tools employed are standard engineering formulae and analytical shell theories; no empirical model training or physical prototypes are required. A safety factor of ≥4.0 against material failure is universally applied.

