1. Geometry and Kinematics

2.1 Local Effective Gravity
At a point on the paraboloid with coordinates (r, \theta, y(r)), the tangent plane is perpendicular to \mathbf{g}_{\text{net}}. The magnitude of the net gravity is

g_{\text{net}}(r) = \sqrt{g_M^2 + (\omega^2 r)^2}.

The local floor slope angle \phi (from the horizontal) satisfies \sin\phi = \omega^2 r / g_{\text{net}}, \cos\phi = g_M / g_{\text{net}}. The local curvature radii are:

R_\phi = \frac{\bigl[1 + (dy/dr)^2\bigr]^{3/2}}{|d^2y/dr^2|} = \frac{[1 + (2kr)^2]^{3/2}}{2k},
\qquad
R_\theta = \frac{r}{\sin\phi} = r \frac{g_{\text{net}}}{\omega^2 r} = \frac{g_{\text{net}}}{\omega^2}.

2.2 Coriolis Acceleration
For any velocity \mathbf{v} relative to the rotating bowl, the Coriolis acceleration is \mathbf{a}_{\text{cor}} = -2\boldsymbol{\omega}\times\mathbf{v}. In a local right‑handed coordinate system [\hat{\mathbf{e}}_r, \hat{\mathbf{e}}_t, \hat{\mathbf{n}}] (radial outward, tangential counter‑rotation, normal outward), with \boldsymbol{\omega} = \omega\,\hat{\mathbf{n}}, the components are:

a_{\text{cor},r} = 2\omega v_t, \qquad
a_{\text{cor},t} = -2\omega v_r, \qquad
a_{\text{cor},n} = 0.

Typical human motions:

· Walking radially at v_r = 1.5\,\text{m/s} → tangential Coriolis acceleration ≈1.28\,\text{m/s}^2 (lateral).
· Walking tangentially at v_t = 1.5\,\text{m/s} → radial Coriolis ≈1.28\,\text{m/s}^2, reducing apparent gravity briefly to 0.87\,g_E.
  These values are sub‑threshold for disorientation given the large radius [1].

