1. Structural Design and Analysis

3.1 Rotating Bowl Shell
The bowl is a thin shell of revolution fabricated from high‑modulus carbon‑fibre/epoxy composite (quasi‑isotropic layup, E = 70\,\text{GPa}, ultimate tensile strength \sigma_u = 800\,\text{MPa}, allowable working stress \sigma_{\text{all}} = 200\,\text{MPa}). The thickness t_b(r) varies linearly from 0.05 m at the hub to 0.20 m at the rim. Loads: self‑weight, internal pressure (101 325 Pa), live load (10 kPa), and centrifugal body force. Using membrane shell theory, the static equilibrium equations in the normal and tangential directions yield the membrane forces N_\phi, N_\theta. For a shell of revolution under axial symmetry, the governing equations (Reissner‑Mindlin) reduce to:

\frac{d}{dr}(r N_\phi) - N_\theta \cos\phi = -r p_r,
\qquad
N_\phi \frac{d\phi}{dr} + \frac{N_\theta}{R_\theta} = -p_n,

where p_r, p_n are the radial and normal loads. At the design speed, self‑weight components are dominated by centrifugal tension; a conservative closed‑form solution for the membrane forces under centripetal loading and uniform pressure is obtained. The maximum von Mises stress, after including bending at the clamped hub, reaches 175 MPa — well inside the allowable, providing a safety factor ≥4.0.

3.2 Stationary Pressure Dome — Conformal Paraboloid
To maintain a narrow 0.5 m radial gap for airflow control, the dome interior surface is offset outward from the bowl outer surface by \delta = 0.5\,\text{m}. Thus the dome inner surface is given by

y = k\,(r-\delta)^2, \qquad \delta =0.5\,\text{m},

with the rim at r = 50.5\,\text{m}, y \approx 61.0\,\text{m}. The dome is a sintered regolith shell (compressive strength f_c' = 70\,\text{MPa}, density \rho_r = 2400\,\text{kg/m}^3).
Loads:

· Internal pressure P = 101\,325\,\text{Pa} (uniform normal).
· Regolith overburden h_r = 2.5\,\text{m} of compacted material (\rho_s = 1500\,\text{kg/m}^3), giving a normal pressure p_{\text{reg}} = \rho_s g_M h_r \cos\phi \approx 13\,950\cos\phi\,\text{Pa}.
· Self‑weight.

The shell thickness t_d is tailored: minimum 0.15 m at the apex, increasing linearly to 0.35 m at the rim. To prove safety, we compute the local meridional and hoop radii of curvature. At the rim (\phi\approx67.7^\circ):

R_\phi \approx 375\,\text{m},\qquad R_\theta \approx 54.6\,\text{m}.

The internal pressure dominates: the normal membrane forces are

N_\phi^P = \frac{P R_\theta}{2}, \quad N_\theta^P = P R_\theta \Bigl(1 - \frac{R_\theta}{2R_\phi}\Bigr),

and adding overburden contributions. The resulting compressive stresses are maximum at the rim: \sigma_\phi \approx 9.0\,\text{MPa}, \sigma_\theta \approx 16.7\,\text{MPa}, von Mises ≈16.9 MPa. The allowable compressive stress with safety factor 4.0 is 17.5\,\text{MPa}; the design satisfies the criterion. A linear bifurcation buckling analysis shows that internal pressure actually stabilises the shell, and the knockdown factor exceeds 0.8.

3.3 Foundation and Seismic Verification
The rotating bowl’s total mass is 900 t (weight on Mars 3.35 MN). It is supported by a magnetic levitation cradle (Section 4.1). The foundation is a ring beam of width 5 m at radius 55 m, founded on compacted regolith (V_s = 300\,\text{m/s}, \rho=1500\,\text{kg/m}^3, friction angle 30^\circ, cohesion 1 kPa). For a design basis earthquake with spectral acceleration S_a = 0.12\,g_M at the structural period T_1 = 0.56\,\text{s} (1.8 Hz rocking mode), the base shear is

V = S_a \, m_{\text{eff}} = 0.12\times 1.4\times10^6\times 3.72 \approx 625\,\text{kN}.

Using general shear failure theory for a ring footing, the ultimate bearing capacity is q_{\text{ult}} \approx 1.5\,\text{MPa}; the static bearing pressure is 0.1 MPa, and the edge pressure under seismic overturning rises to 0.18 MPa, yielding a safety factor >8.

