1. Magnetic Levitation and Spin‑Up Drive

4.1 Inductrack Magnetic Bearing
The rotating bowl rim is equipped with a Halbach array of NdFeB permanent magnets (remanence B_r = 1.42\,\text{T}). The array moves over a stationary laminated aluminium track. Following the analytical model of Post and Ryutov, the magnetic field above the array is

B_y(x,y) = B_0\, e^{-k y} \sin(k x),

with k = 2\pi/\lambda, \lambda the magnet wavelength. The time‑varying flux induces eddy currents, producing a lift force per unit length

F_L' = \frac{B_0^2 w}{2\mu_0} \frac{v}{v + v_0},

and a drag force F_D' = F_L' \, (v_0/v) at low speeds, where v = \omega r_{\text{array}}. By choosing appropriate Halbach geometry (\lambda \approx 0.3\,\text{m}, width w=1.2\,\text{m}), we obtain a total lift of 4.2 MN at the operational speed v\approx21.3\,\text{m/s}, exceeding the bowl weight.

The restoring centering force due to lateral displacement \Delta x is analytically approximated by a reluctance force linearisation:

F_{\text{center}} = -\,k_c \Delta x,

with k_c \approx 4.0\times10^7\,\text{N/m} determined from magnetic circuit analysis. This yields a lateral natural frequency of \sqrt{k_c/m} \approx 2.3\,\text{Hz}.

4.2 Spin‑Up Motor
The moment of inertia of the bowl plus water ballast is I = 1.2\times10^9\,\text{kg·m}^2. The target angular acceleration \dot{\omega} = 0.426 / 1800 = 2.367\times10^{-4}\,\text{rad/s}^2 (spin‑up time 30 min). Required torque:

\tau = I \dot{\omega} = 2.84\times10^5\,\text{N·m}.

Peak power at full speed: P = \tau \omega \approx 121\,\text{kW}. A 150 kW three‑phase induction motor with a 30:1 planetary gearbox supplies the necessary starting torque (3.0 × 10⁵ N·m). The motor disengages via a splined clutch once the bowl reaches operating speed; thereafter a small 2 kW trim motor compensates the magnetic drag torque of ≈200 N·m.

4.3 Slosh Dynamics
Each ballast tank (volume 5 m³, radial length 4 m) is modelled as a linear oscillator with first slosh frequency \omega_s = \sqrt{g_{\text{eff}}/L}, where L is the free‑surface length. By inserting high‑porosity baffles, the effective length is reduced, raising \omega_s above 1.2 Hz, far from the rotation frequency. The resulting slosh forces remain within the controller’s rejection bandwidth (see Section 5).

