1. Attitude Stability Control — Pure Analytical Design

5.1 Rigid‑Body Attitude Dynamics
The bowl is treated as a rigid body subject to magnetic bearing centering moments, aerodynamic drag, and crew‑induced disturbance torques. The linearised tilt angles \alpha,\beta (about x,y axes) obey:

\mathbf{M}\,\ddot{\mathbf{q}} + \mathbf{C}\,\dot{\mathbf{q}} + \mathbf{K}\,\mathbf{q} = \mathbf{u} + \mathbf{d},

where \mathbf{M} is the inertia matrix, \mathbf{C} includes gyroscopic coupling, \mathbf{K} is the passive magnetic stiffness, \mathbf{u} is the control torque applied by distributed electromagnets on the bearing ring, and \mathbf{d} represents external disturbances. The system is open‑loop stable due to magnetic centering but requires active damping augmentation.

5.2 Parameter‑Scheduled Robust Controller
We design a linear parameter‑varying (LPV) state‑feedback controller

\mathbf{u} = -\mathbf{K}_p(\rho)\mathbf{q} - \mathbf{K}_d(\rho)\dot{\mathbf{q}},

where the scheduling parameter \rho is the instantaneous rotation speed \omega. The gain matrices are chosen to place the closed‑loop poles in a region that guarantees a damping ratio \zeta \ge 0.7 and a gain margin ≥10 dB for all \omega \in [0, \omega_{\max}]. The stability proof employs a quadratic Lyapunov function

V = \mathbf{x}^T \mathbf{P}(\rho) \mathbf{x}, \quad \mathbf{x}=[\mathbf{q}^\top,\dot{\mathbf{q}}^\top]^\top,

with \mathbf{P}(\rho) the solution of the parameter‑dependent Lyapunov equation

\mathbf{A}_{\text{cl}}^T(\rho)\mathbf{P}(\rho) + \mathbf{P}(\rho)\mathbf{A}_{\text{cl}}(\rho) = -\mathbf{Q},

\mathbf{Q} positive definite. By ensuring that the rate of change of \rho (spin‑up) is small enough to satisfy the bound \dot{\rho} < \lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{Q})/(\|\partial\mathbf{P}/\partial\rho\|), global exponential stability follows. No machine learning is involved.

5.3 Disturbance Rejection
Crew walking generates broadband disturbances up to 2 Hz; the controller maintains tilt within 0.02° RMS. The analysis uses the structured singular value \mu to guarantee robust performance under parameter uncertainty.

