1. Interior Atmospheric Management

6.1 Taylor‑Couette Flow Suppression
The 0.5 m radial gap between the rotating bowl and the stationary dome constitutes a narrow annulus. The angular velocity ratio \eta = 0 (stationary outer wall) yields a critical Taylor number for the onset of azimuthal vortices (laminar‑Taylor‑vortex transition) of Ta_c \approx 1700. The operating Taylor number

Ta = \frac{4\,\omega^2 R_{\text{in}}^2 \delta^3}{\nu^2 (1-\eta^4)}

with R_{\text{in}} \approx 50\,\text{m}, \delta = 0.5\,\text{m}, \nu \approx 1.5\times10^{-5}\,\text{m}^2/\text{s} (air) gives Ta \approx 6.4\times10^{10}, far exceeding critical—indicating fully turbulent flow. To suppress large‑scale spiral wind, we install stationary annular porous baffles (porosity 40%) at vertical intervals of h=5\,\text{m}. Based on the theory of screened Taylor‑Couette flow, these baffles break the azimuthal momentum exchange and restrict large‑eddy circulation. With baffles, the maximum tangential velocity in the occupied zone is analytically bounded by U_{\theta,\max} \le 0.1\,\omega R \,(h/R), yielding ~0.3 m/s, imperceptible.

6.2 Visual Mitigation
A static starfield is projected onto the dome interior using a system of fixed projectors; the crew reference frame is non‑rotating. Radial interior walls block the rotating dome periphery, eliminating vection.

