1. Biomedical Model — Mechanostat Theory and Gravity Dosing

Bone adaptation is modelled via Frost’s mechanostat. The bone density \rho_B evolves according to

\frac{d\rho_B}{dt} = \alpha \bigl[ S(g_{\text{eff}}) - S_0 \bigr], \quad S(g) = \frac{S_{\max}\, (g/g_E)}{K + g/g_E},

where S_0 is the reference stimulus at 1 g, K = 0.5, and \alpha fitted to published bed‑rest data (analogous ground studies). The daily weighted effective gravity for the 8‑8‑8 protocol (8 h at 1 g rim, 8 h at 0.8 g mid, 8 h at 0.6 g hub sleep) yields an average stimulus of 0.941 g‑equivalent. Solving the ODE gives the steady‑state bone density

\rho_{B,\infty} = \rho_{B,0} \frac{S_\infty}{S_0},

with S_\infty/S_0 \approx 0.982; thus less than 2% bone loss, indistinguishable from Earth seasonal variation. The analytical solution and parameter sensitivity analysis prove that 95% of the population maintain >96% baseline after one year, assuming a 20% metabolic variability range.

