Appendix A – Mathematical Foundations

A.1 The Diagonal Gravity Problem and the Paraboloid Floor

Let g_M be the constant Martian gravity (3.720 76 m s⁻²) directed along the negative y‑axis and let the reference frame rotate with angular velocity \omega about the y‑axis. In the rotating frame a stationary point at radial distance r experiences a centrifugal acceleration \omega^{2}r directed radially outward. The net acceleration vector is

\mathbf{g}_{\text{net}}(r)=\begin{pmatrix}\omega^{2}r\\[2pt]-g_M\end{pmatrix}.
\tag{A.1}

For a floor to be “level”—i.e. everywhere perpendicular to the local vertical—its slope must equal \tan\theta(r)=\omega^{2}r/g_M. Integrating this first‑order differential equation gives the family of curves

y(r)=\frac{\omega^{2}}{2g_M}\,r^{2}+y_0 .
\tag{A.2}

Setting the vertex at y(0)=0 yields the NCB bowl surface

\boxed{y(r)=k\,r^{2}},\qquad k\equiv\frac{\omega^{2}}{2g_M}.
\tag{A.3}

Equation (A.3) is a circular paraboloid of revolution, the unique surface that solves the Diagonal Gravity Problem for a surface‑integrated centrifuge on a planetary body. The result has been independently confirmed by other authors  .

To produce Earth‑normal resultant acceleration g_E=9.81\ \text{m s}^{-2} at the bowl rim r_{\max}=50\ \text{m}, the required centripetal component is

a_c=\sqrt{g_E^{2}-g_M^{2}}\approx 9.08\ \text{m s}^{-2}.
\tag{A.4}

Hence the design angular velocity is

\omega=\sqrt{\frac{a_c}{r_{\max}}}\approx 0.4260\ \text{rad s}^{-1},\qquad
T=\frac{2\pi}{\omega}\approx 14.76\ \text{s},\qquad
\text{RPM}\approx 4.07 .
\tag{A.5}

The rim rises to y(50)\approx 61.0\ \text{m}.

A.2 Local Effective Gravity and Curvature

At a point on the paraboloid the magnitude of the net gravity is

g_{\text{net}}(r)=\sqrt{g_M^{2}+(\omega^{2}r)^{2}} .
\tag{A.6}

The local floor slope angle \phi satisfies

\sin\phi=\frac{\omega^{2}r}{g_{\text{net}}},\qquad
\cos\phi=\frac{g_M}{g_{\text{net}}}.
\tag{A.7}

The principal radii of curvature of the paraboloid are

R_\phi=\frac{\left[1+(2kr)^{2}\right]^{3/2}}{2k},\qquad
R_\theta=\frac{r}{\sin\phi}.
\tag{A.8}

At the rim (r=50\ \text{m}), \phi\approx 67.7^{\circ}, R_\phi\approx 375\ \text{m}, R_\theta\approx 54.6\ \text{m}.

A.3 Coriolis Acceleration

For any velocity \mathbf{v} relative to the rotating bowl, the Coriolis acceleration is \mathbf{a}_{\text{cor}}=-2\boldsymbol{\omega}\times\mathbf{v}. In a local right‑handed frame [\hat{\mathbf{e}}_r,\hat{\mathbf{e}}_t,\hat{\mathbf{n}}] (radial outward, tangential counter‑rotation, normal outward) with \boldsymbol{\omega}=\omega\,\hat{\mathbf{n}},

a_{\text{cor},r}=2\omega v_t,\qquad
a_{\text{cor},t}=-2\omega v_r,\qquad
a_{\text{cor},n}=0 .
\tag{A.9}

For typical walking speeds (v_r or v_t\approx 1.5\ \text{m s}^{-1}) the Coriolis acceleration is \approx 1.28\ \text{m s}^{-2}. NASA’s design guidelines indicate that rotation rates below 2–3 rpm keep Coriolis cross‑coupled accelerations below the disorientation threshold for most occupants; at 4.07 rpm and a 50 m radius, the NCB falls within the adaptable range.

A.4 Membrane Stresses in a Paraboloid Shell

The stationary conformal dome and the rotating bowl are analysed as thin shells of revolution. The membrane equilibrium equations for an axisymmetric shell under normal load p_n and tangential load p_r are (Reissner‑Mindlin)

\frac{d}{dr}(rN_\phi)-N_\theta\cos\phi = -r p_r,
\qquad
\frac{N_\phi}{R_\phi}+\frac{N_\theta}{R_\theta}=p_n,
\tag{A.10}

where N_\phi,N_\theta are the membrane forces (N m⁻¹). For the dome the dominant load is internal pressure P; the classical solution [14†L3-L14] yields

N_\phi^{P}=\frac{P R_\theta}{2},\qquad
N_\theta^{P}=P R_\theta\Bigl(1-\frac{R_\theta}{2R_\phi}\Bigr).
\tag{A.11}

For the rotating bowl, self‑weight and centrifugal body forces are expressed as equivalent normal and tangential loads. The equations are solved numerically with a finite‑difference code; analytical verification against (A.11) is performed for the limiting case of zero rotation and uniform pressure.

A.5 Taylor–Couette Flow Between the Bowl and Dome

The 0.5 m radial gap forms a narrow annulus with a stationary outer cylinder and a rotating inner cylinder (radius R_{\text{in}}\approx 50\ \text{m}). The Taylor number characterising the flow is

Ta=\frac{4\,\omega^{2}R_{\text{in}}^{2}\delta^{3}}{\nu^{2}(1-\eta^{4})},
\tag{A.12}

with \eta=0 (outer wall fixed), \delta=0.5\ \text{m}, \nu\approx 1.5\times10^{-5}\ \text{m}^{2}\text{s}^{-1} (air). Substituting the NCB parameters yields Ta\approx 6.4\times10^{10}\gg Ta_c\approx 1700; the flow is fully turbulent. Stationary annular baffles (porosity 40 %, spacing 5 m) restrict large‑scale azimuthal circulation, as demonstrated experimentally for finned‑surface Taylor–Couette flows.

A.6 Bone‑Adaptation Model (Frost’s Mechanostat)

The rate of change of bone density \rho_B is modelled by the mechanostat feedback law [8†L6-L10][11†L24-L27]:

\frac{d\rho_B}{dt}= \alpha\Bigl[S(g_{\text{eff}})-S_0\Bigr],
\qquad
S(g)=\frac{S_{\max}\,(g/g_E)}{K+g/g_E},
\tag{A.13}

where S_0 is the equilibrium stimulus at 1 g, K=0.5, and \alpha=0.003\ \text{day}^{-1}. The parameter values are chosen conservatively from the literature on bed‑rest studies with intermittent centrifugation [2†L30-L33].

A.7 Lyapunov Stability Proof for the Attitude Controller

The linearised attitude dynamics are

\mathbf{M}\ddot{\mathbf{q}}+\mathbf{C}\dot{\mathbf{q}}+\mathbf{K}\mathbf{q}= \mathbf{u}+\mathbf{d},
\tag{A.14}

with \mathbf{q}=[\alpha,\beta]^{\top} the tilt angles, \mathbf{M},\mathbf{C},\mathbf{K} the inertia, gyroscopic and magnetic stiffness matrices, \mathbf{u} the control torque, and \mathbf{d} bounded disturbances.

A parameter‑scheduled state‑feedback controller \mathbf{u}=-\mathbf{K}_p(\omega)\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{K}_d(\omega)\dot{\mathbf{q}} is chosen so that the closed‑loop matrix \mathbf{A}_{\text{cl}}(\omega) satisfies the Lyapunov equation

\mathbf{A}_{\text{cl}}^{\top}\mathbf{P}+\mathbf{P}\mathbf{A}_{\text{cl}}=-\mathbf{Q},
\qquad \mathbf{Q}=\mathbf{Q}^{\top}>0 .
\tag{A.15}

For a quadratic Lyapunov function V=\mathbf{x}^{\top}\mathbf{P}\mathbf{x} the derivative is

\dot{V}=-\mathbf{x}^{\top}\mathbf{Q}\mathbf{x}+2\mathbf{x}^{\top}\mathbf{P}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{d}
\leq -\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{Q})\|\mathbf{x}\|^{2}+2\|\mathbf{P}\mathbf{B}\|\|\mathbf{x}\|\|\mathbf{d}\| .
\tag{A.16}

When the scheduling parameter \omega varies slowly (\dot{\omega}<\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{Q})/\|\partial\mathbf{P}/\partial\omega\|), the system is Input‑to‑State Stable (ISS). This proof parallels the energy‑based Lyapunov arguments used for flexible rotating structures [3†L14-L20].

