The dome is covered by a 2.5 m regolith radiation shield.

B.2 Magnetic Levitation and Drive

Inductrack bearing: A Halbach array of NdFeB permanent magnets (remanence B_r=1.42\ \text{T}, wavelength \lambda=0.3\ \text{m}, width 1.2 m) is mounted on the bowl rim. The array moves over a continuous laminated aluminium track fixed to the foundation. The lift‑to‑drag ratio exceeds 100:1 at operational speed (21.3 m s⁻¹), providing 4.2 MN total lift—25 % greater than the bowl weight. The passive centering stiffness is k_c=4.0\times10^{7}\ \text{N m}^{-1}, corresponding to a lateral natural frequency of 2.3 Hz.

Spin‑up motor: A 150 kW induction motor with a 30:1 planetary gearbox delivers 3.0\times10^{5}\ \text{N·m} starting torque. The bowl accelerates from rest to 4.07 rpm in 30 min (\dot{\omega}=2.367\times10^{-4}\ \text{rad s}^{-2}). At operating speed the drive clutch disengages; magnetic drag (~200 N·m) is compensated by a 2 kW trim motor.

B.3 Attitude Stability Controller

The LPV state‑feedback controller is implemented with gain matrices stored for discrete values of \omega (step 0.005 rad s⁻¹). Four electromagnets on the bearing ring provide the control torques. The controller maintains tilt below 0.02° RMS under crew‑induced disturbances up to 2 Hz.

B.4 Interior Atmosphere

The 0.5 m conformal gap is fitted with stationary porous annular baffles every 5 m vertically. The baffles limit tangential air velocity in the occupied zone to <0.3 m s⁻¹. The dome interior surface carries a fixed starfield projection; radial walls block the rotating dome periphery.

B.5 Water and Waste Management

The bowl floor is a continuous paraboloid with a micro‑texture (0.5° radial slope) that guides all liquids to a grated sump at the central hub. A single drain line runs to treatment. Toilets use centrifugal separation; solids are pneumatically transported. Water‑glycol loops in the bowl inner skin maintain cabin temperature at 22 °C.

B.6 Power System

A bank of four Kilopower fission reactors provides 40 kWe continuous power. The reactors are sited 500 m from the habitat. Electrical power is transmitted across the rotating interface via an inductive coupler (efficiency 95 %).

B.7 Life Support (ECLSS)

Closed‑loop life support combines:

· Sabatier reactor (CO₂ + 4 H₂ → CH₄ + 2 H₂O) with imported hydrogen,
· 4‑bed molecular sieve for CO₂ removal,
· Vapour‑compression distillation for water recovery,
· Oxygen generation by electrolysis.

Stationary ECLSS equipment is mounted on the dome interior; quick‑disconnect interfaces cross the magnetic bearing gap.

B.8 Emergency Systems

